s keep coming. Record lows have nearly stopped.","p_rec":"If Colorado Springs' climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Colorado Springs has set <strong class=\"sig\">30 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 75 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">198 record highs</strong>, far more than the 75 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (82). Colorado Springs' all-time high is 101\u00b0F, on June 26, 2012.","t_chg":"Colorado Springs has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 126 complete years: Colorado Springs' average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.20\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +0.25), about +2.6\u00b0F since 1895. Colorado Springs' average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.29\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.21 to +0.37), about +3.8\u00b0F since 1895.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 8 of 12 months. Highs rose in 8 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Colorado Springs has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Colorado Springs' yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.21 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.02 to +0.41), about +2.8 inches since 1895. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 15.9 inches a year. The wettest year was 1999 (27.58 in); the driest, 1939 (6.07 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 18 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}